const heraclitusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'An ordered world recovered from scattered words', [
    'Heraclitus of Ephesus was active around 500 BCE and survives through brief passages quoted or summarized by authors with their own philosophical purposes. His modern image is therefore built from several layers: wording plausibly preserved from his composition, the framing supplied by the citer, ancient summaries of a supposed doctrine, and modern editorial arrangement. These layers cannot be made identical. The surviving material nevertheless has a recognizable center. It repeatedly joins a common logos, measured transformations, opposed terms, human failures of understanding, and the demand to attend to a world whose order is public rather than privately invented.',
    'The familiar label “philosopher of flux” captures only one reception. Heraclitus uses rivers and fire, yet he also speaks of measure, commonality, law, and connections that hold wholes together. Radical instability without pattern would make those themes unintelligible. Conversely, treating him as a simple material monist whose one substance is fire neglects his language of exchange and transformation. The best orientation is a problem rather than a slogan: how can identity, difference, conflict, and intelligibility belong to one world? His compressed prose makes readers perform the work of connecting examples without pretending that one later systematic formula exhausts them.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'metaphysics', 'epistemology'], ['parmenides', 'plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments']),
  section('transmission', 'Quotation, paraphrase, and edition-dependent fragments', [
    'No manuscript of a Heraclitean book survives. Most direct-looking fragments are embedded in writers from Plato and Aristotle through Stoic, skeptical, medical, and Christian traditions, while Diogenes Laertius supplies a late life assembled largely from anecdotes. Even a grammatically complete sentence may have been shortened, adapted, or detached from an argument. Kahn’s edition marks a difference between likely verbatim citations and mixed reports; other editions draw boundaries differently. “Fragment” thus names a modern editorial category, not a scrap carrying its own authentication. A review must identify the transmitting witness and avoid lending a report the status of Heraclitus’s own continuous prose.',
    'Numbering is equally mediated. Traditional references such as DK 22 B1 or B12 remain useful because scholarship widely recognizes them, but Kahn and Laks–Most arrange and number the evidence according to different principles. A bare “fragment 1” is therefore ambiguous. This article uses topics in its prose and gives concordance-friendly traditional numbers in evidence locators, without implying that the DK order reconstructs an authorial sequence. The ancient claim that Heraclitus deposited a book in Artemis’s temple and later tripartite divisions into cosmology, politics, and theology belong to reception history. They do not securely establish the original title, order, or architecture of a finished treatise.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], [], ['Fragments']),
  section('life-ephesus', 'Ephesus, chronology, and a biography made from sayings', [
    'Heraclitus was associated with Ephesus, an Ionian city under Persian power in his lifetime. His own criticism of Pythagoras, Xenophanes, and Hecataeus helps place him near the turn of the sixth to fifth century BCE, while ancient synchronisms yield an approximate flourishing around 500. Exact birth and death years are not recoverable. Stories that he inherited a royal office, refused legislation, withdrew from civic life, lived on plants, developed dropsy, or died through an eccentric treatment are late and often look like narrative expansions of themes in the fragments. They reveal how antiquity imagined an obscure critic more reliably than they document his life.',
    'Some fragments do engage civic judgment directly. Heraclitus condemns the expulsion of Hermodorus, distrusts the uncomprehending many, and links a city’s law with a more encompassing divine order. These remarks justify describing a severe political and ethical voice, but not a complete constitutional program or a certified aristocratic career. Ephesus supplies relevant context: public law, faction, Persian rule, cult, poetry, and Ionian natural inquiry formed part of his world. His attacks on crowds and celebrated teachers should be read as claims about failed understanding, not converted automatically into every biographical trait that later storytellers assigned to him.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'political-philosophy'], ['pythagoras', 'xenophanes'], ['Fragments']),
  section('logos', 'Logos between speech, account, proportion, and reality', [
    'Logos is indispensable and resistant to a single English substitute. Depending on context it can mean something said, an account, reasoning, a measure, or a relation. In the opening fragment, people fail to understand a logos even though events occur according to it; elsewhere an account is common although people act as if their thinking were private. The word therefore links Heraclitus’s discourse, an intelligible pattern in events, and the activity of understanding. Translating it once as “reason” risks importing a later cosmic substance, while translating it merely as “speech” can hide the claim that what is said answers to how things happen.',
    'The fragments do not securely state a later Stoic doctrine of a providential rational principle pervading matter. Stoics found Heraclitus congenial and helped shape his reception, but their technical logos should not be projected backward unchanged. Nor does “common” mean immediately obvious or democratically agreed. The common account can be missed by nearly everyone, including well-informed people, because recognizing it requires more than collecting facts. Heraclitus connects inquiry with listening, self-search, comparison, and discernment. Logos is best treated as the shared intelligibility that discourse tries to articulate, while its exact metaphysical status remains contested.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-language', 'epistemology'], [], ['Fragments']),
  section('flux-rivers', 'Rivers, change, and the afterlife of “everything flows”', [
    'The best-supported river wording says that different waters flow upon those who step into the same rivers. The sentence holds sameness and replacement together: without changing waters there is no river of this kind, but without an identifiable river there is nothing into which one steps. Other river formulations, including claims about stepping twice or both stepping and not stepping, reach us through later witnesses and have disputed textual status. The slogan panta rhei, “everything flows,” is not securely preserved as Heraclitus’s own sentence. It is a compact emblem of a later interpretation whose scope must be argued rather than assumed.',
    'Plato’s Cratylus associates Heraclitus with everything moving, and the Theaetetus develops a broad flux theory in an epistemological argument. Aristotle likewise treats him as threatening non-contradiction. These texts are essential evidence for early reception, especially through figures such as Cratylus, but they are not neutral transcripts. Heraclitus certainly emphasizes transformation, exchange, and temporal succession. Whether every property of every object changes at every moment is much less secure. A measured interpretation sees stability as organized process: identity can depend on regulated renewal, while knowledge concerns the pattern and measures of change rather than a frozen thing outside all becoming.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Cratylus', 'Theaetetus']),
  section('opposites', 'Opposites connected without careless contradiction', [
    'Heraclitus places opposites beside one another in several ways. The same sea is healthful for fish and harmful for humans because the relation differs; one person moves through waking and sleeping, youth and age, because states succeed and transform into one another; a road can be described as up or down depending on direction; a bow or lyre works through opposed tension. These examples do not all express one logical relation. Perspective, sequence, dependence, polarity, and functional tension must be distinguished before they are gathered under the modern label “unity of opposites.”',
    'Conflict is consequently not simple chaos or moral approval of every war. Heraclitus calls strife common and connects it with justice, suggesting that differentiated order requires forces that resist and delimit each other. His notorious language about war producing gods and humans, free and enslaved, reflects an archaic world in which conflict assigns positions; it should not be made into an unrestricted ethical endorsement of violence. The philosophical advance lies in seeing that harmony need not mean the elimination of difference. Yet the surviving sayings leave open how cosmic, political, and moral conflict align, and later dialectical interpretations should remain reception rather than recovered doctrine.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'logic', 'ethics'], ['aristotle'], ['Fragments']),
  section('fire-measure', 'Fire, exchange, and a cosmos governed by measures', [
    'Heraclitus describes the world-order as ever-living fire that kindles and goes out according to measures, and compares exchanges between fire and all things with exchanges between goods and gold. Ancient doxography consequently classed him with thinkers who selected one material principle. That classification may preserve an important physical commitment, but it can also force Heraclitus into an Aristotelian history of material causes. Fire is a concrete cosmic stuff, a process that lives through consuming and transforming, and an image of measured exchange. The fragments do not decisively tell us which of those roles is primary or whether the alternatives would have been separate for him.',
    'Measure prevents the fire doctrine from collapsing into random combustion. Transformations among fire, sea, earth, storm, or soul occur in proportions and reciprocal directions, though the surviving evidence does not give a complete physical mechanism. Reports of a periodic world conflagration are especially contested because Stoic cosmology may have guided the reading. Some scholars infer an eternal ordered cosmos without total destruction; others find stronger cyclical implications. The safe claim is narrower: fire makes transformation central, exchange makes apparently different things commensurable, and measure makes change intelligible. A full Heraclitean cosmological cycle cannot be reconstructed with the confidence of the surviving formulations themselves.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['aristotle', 'zeno'], ['Fragments']),
  section('knowledge-soul', 'Learning, soul, and the discipline of attention', [
    'Heraclitus attacks polymathy because extensive learning did not teach understanding to celebrated predecessors. This is not a rejection of inquiry: he reports searching himself, distinguishes direct experience from mere hearsay, and demands that listeners recognize a common account. The target is accumulation without synthesis. Human beings can inhabit a shared waking world while living cognitively as though asleep, mistaking private impression, fame, or inherited authority for insight. His epistemology is therefore ethical in tone. Becoming capable of understanding requires attention, comparison, and a willingness to be corrected by patterns that do not depend on one person’s preference.',
    'Fragments about soul deepen that connection but resist a finished psychology. Soul has a logos whose limits cannot easily be found; drunkenness moistens it and impairs action; a dry soul is praised in text whose exact wording is disputed. These images connect cognition, bodily condition, and self-command without establishing a modern immaterial mind. Soul appears within elemental transformations and may be fiery, but the evidence does not yield one complete physiology or eschatology. Heraclitus makes the knower part of nature: understanding the cosmos is not detached spectatorship, and a disordered life can damage the capacities by which common order becomes intelligible.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['socrates'], ['Fragments']),
  section('divine-law', 'God, law, justice, and human perspective', [
    'Divine language runs through the fragments, but it does not form a conventional theology. Heraclitus speaks of one wise thing, Zeus and not-Zeus, divine law nourishing human laws, and contrasts between human and divine understanding. Such formulations can expose the inadequacy of inherited divine names while retaining a sacred dimension to cosmic order. The one wise is not straightforwardly a personal creator outside the cosmos, and logos is not automatically identical with a later providential God. The fragments instead pressure readers to compare limited human evaluations with a wider pattern in which opposites and transformations have places that local perspectives cannot see.',
    'This difference of perspective complicates justice. A deity may regard a wider order as just while humans classify some things as just and others unjust; yet Heraclitus also criticizes civic decisions and urges defense of law. The result is not moral nihilism. Human laws and judgments are answerable to something more common, but the route from cosmic regularity to a specific political command is not preserved. Later natural-law and Stoic readings develop one possibility. A cautious account identifies the connection among common reason, measure, and law while refusing to claim that Heraclitus supplied a systematic jurisprudence or solved the problem of deriving human obligations from nature.',
  ], ['ethics', 'political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['Fragments']),
  section('reception', 'Plato, Aristotle, Stoics, and competing Heraclitean portraits', [
    'Plato established the most influential portrait by linking Heraclitus with pervasive flux and with followers who made stable discourse difficult. Aristotle added a material-principle narrative and treated Heraclitean opposites in relation to non-contradiction. Stoics found precedents for fiery cosmology, logos, and cosmic law. Each reception preserved evidence while selecting it through new problems. Heraclitus consequently became several canonical figures: radical flux theorist, material monist, obscure paradox-maker, proto-Stoic, dialectician, process philosopher, and religious critic. None can simply be substituted for the fragments; each should be tested against their wording, transmission, and range.',
    'Influence claims also require chronological restraint. Heraclitus and Parmenides are often staged as direct opposites, but whether either responded to the other is disputed. Empedocles echoes themes that may be Heraclitean, and later Hippocratic or Democritean texts share formulations, yet a secure teacher-to-pupil chain is unavailable. Modern appropriations by Hegel, Nietzsche, and Heidegger are significant reception rather than evidence for original meaning. Heraclitus’s durable achievement needs no invented lineage: he made ordered transformation, relational opposition, shared intelligibility, and the moral difficulty of understanding into problems that later philosophy repeatedly reconfigured.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'stoicism', 'metaphysics'], ['parmenides', 'plato', 'aristotle', 'empedocles'], ['Cratylus', 'Theaetetus', 'Metaphysics']),
  section('reading', 'A method for reading without flattening the fragments', [
    'Begin with an edition that identifies the transmitting author and provides concordances among DK, Kahn, and Laks–Most numbering. Mark direct quotation, paraphrase, and doxography separately. Read clusters on logos, rivers, opposites, fire and measure, soul, and law, then ask what kind of connection each passage actually asserts. Do not let the shortest English slogan decide the Greek word’s full range. Comparing two translations is especially useful for logos, harmoniē, kosmos, and psychē because a smooth equivalent can conceal whether the source concerns discourse, proportion, world-order, or embodied cognitive life.',
    'Only after that first pass should Plato’s Cratylus and Theaetetus, Aristotle’s Metaphysics, and Diogenes’s life enter the notebook. Label them reception, analysis, or biography rather than “more fragments.” Kahn offers a powerful literary and systematic arrangement; the SEP article tests major interpretations and highlights their conflicts; Laks–Most supplies a newer evidence organization. The goal is not to escape interpretation, which fragmented transmission makes impossible. It is to keep every reconstruction revisable. A responsible beginner can conclude that Heraclitus explores intelligible order through change and opposition while still leaving logos, universal flux, fire, and unity genuinely contested.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Cratylus', 'Theaetetus', 'Metaphysics']),
];

