const empedoclesSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'A poetic inquiry spanning cosmos and conduct', [
    'Empedocles of Acragas wrote hexameter poetry that investigates matter, cosmic history, living bodies, perception, ritual pollution, exile, and the prospect of a transformed life. Four enduring roots—commonly identified as earth, water, air, and fire—combine and separate under Love and Strife. That physical vocabulary answers the prohibition on generation from nothing, yet the surviving poems also speak in the voice of a fallen daimōn and condemn forms of killing. Modern divisions between science, religion, medicine, and poetry help organize his topics but can distort a project in which understanding nature and changing one’s conduct remain entangled.',
    'There is no harmless one-sentence system. The roots are stable, but composites are born and die conventionally; Love joins unlike roots, yet a complete victory of Love may erase differentiated creatures; Strife separates, yet separation can also group like with like. A cosmic cycle is widely accepted, while its exact phases and the number of cosmogonies remain disputed. Religious fragments may illuminate the physical theory without fitting every detail into it. Empedocles matters precisely because his poetry sustains these tensions: a pluralist ontology, an ambitious natural history, and a moral account of kinship are preserved in fragments whose original architecture cannot simply be restored.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'metaphysics', 'ethics'], ['parmenides', 'anaxagoras'], ['On Nature', 'Purifications']),
  section('transmission-poems', 'Fragments, papyri, and the one-poem/two-poem problem', [
    'Ancient testimony commonly names a physical poem, often called On Nature, and Purifications, and sometimes assigns them separate line totals. Modern editors long sorted cosmological material into the first and religious material into the second. Yet no complete medieval manuscript survives. Hundreds of verses come from quotations, paraphrases, and reports distributed across later authors. The Strasbourg papyrus added direct manuscript portions of the physical poem and placed material about daimones near cosmic material, renewing arguments for a single poetic project or at least a closer relation between the conventional works. Neither one-poem nor two-poem reconstruction is established beyond dispute.',
    'The evidential landscape is still changing. P. Strasb. gr. Inv. 1665–1666 overlaps previously known lines and adds sequences important for reconstructing the cycle. In 2026, scholars announced and published P. Fouad inv. 218 from Cairo, preserving thirty previously unknown verses associated with the Physica and the same ancient roll tradition. This profile records that discovery without using a press summary as support for new doctrinal detail. Traditional DK numbers, Strasbourg ensemble designations, and newer Laks–Most numbers do not align automatically. Every fragment locator is therefore edition-dependent, and an argument about order must name the reconstruction on which it relies.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], [], ['On Nature', 'Purifications', 'Strasbourg papyrus', 'Cairo papyrus']),
  section('life-acragas', 'Acragas and the invention of an extraordinary life', [
    'Empedocles belonged to fifth-century Acragas in Sicily, but conventional dates around 494–434 BCE are approximate. Ancient reports associate his family with wealth and public life and portray him as democratic leader, physician, healer, poet, rhetorician, wonder-worker, or divine claimant. Some activities may preserve memory of an authoritative public intellectual; many anecdotes elaborate his poetic persona. The famous death in Etna appears in mutually incompatible late stories and should not anchor biography. Even claims of direct teacher relations with Pythagoreans, Parmenides, or Gorgias are uncertain rather than entries in a documented institutional lineage.',
    'Western Greek context remains illuminating without settling those tales. Sicily and southern Italy supported medical practice, public performance, Pythagorean traditions, mystery language, colonization, civic conflict, and rival claims to wisdom. Empedocles addresses named or implied audiences with unusual authority, sometimes as teacher to Pausanias and sometimes as a wanderer honored by communities. Those voices belong to poetry and may stage philosophical authority rather than report literal status. The safest life combines the secure place-name Acragas, activity in the mid-fifth century, a reputation broad enough to generate many roles, and explicit uncertainty about political deeds, miracles, travel, chronology, and death.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['pythagoras', 'parmenides', 'gorgias'], ['Purifications']),
  section('roots-generation', 'Four roots and the redescription of birth and death', [
    'Empedocles denies absolute coming-to-be and destruction. What mortals call birth is the mixing of enduring constituents, and what they call death is their separation. This response resembles Eleatic constraints without proving that a specific Parmenidean argument caused every feature of the theory. Empedocles calls the constituents “roots” and invokes divine names when listing them; identifications of individual names with earth, air, fire, and water have been debated, although the four familiar stuffs are secure as a group. Calling them Aristotle’s “elements” is convenient reception vocabulary but risks making them inert materials in a later technical scheme.',
    'Each root has its own character and remains what it is through mixtures. Observable things differ because roots occur in different ratios and arrangements, as painters produce varied figures from a limited palette. This does not mean that Empedocles possessed a quantitative chemistry or modern conservation law. His proportions are explanatory and sometimes highly specific, but the textual evidence is poetic, selective, and transmitted by interested witnesses. The achievement is conceptual: stable plurality replaces one basic stuff, while composition accounts for genuine diversity. A bone, plant, blood, or cosmos is not an illusion, yet its generation need not introduce any root that previously did not exist.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['parmenides', 'aristotle'], ['On Nature']),
  section('love-strife', 'Love and Strife as powers, divinities, and explanatory causes', [
    'Love and Strife move the roots without being reducible to ordinary affection and hostility. Love promotes mixture among unlike roots; Strife loosens those combinations and separates unlike constituents, with like roots consequently gathering. Both are described as enduring realities and as divine powers. Aristotle famously assigns Empedocles an early role in the history of efficient causes and aligns Love with good and Strife with evil, but the poems resist that moral sorting. Organisms require differentiated mixtures produced during contested phases, and total unification or total separation would not preserve the living world familiar to us.',
    'The forces operate at cosmic and bodily scales, but their mechanism is not fully recoverable. They may be spatial ingredients, powers distributed through roots, tendencies of attraction and repulsion, or divine agencies not captured by those alternatives. Translating them as modern physical “forces” is useful only with this warning. Empedocles’s explanatory move is to deny that the roots organize themselves unaided: mixture has a cause, separation has a cause, and neither wins forever. The result is dynamic pluralism. Yet the poetic personification and the ethical language of love and conflict invite connections that should be explored without manufacturing one seamless causal vocabulary.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['aristotle'], ['On Nature']),
  section('cycle', 'The Sphere, separation, and disputed cosmic histories', [
    'Fragment B17 describes a repeated movement from many to one and one to many as Love and Strife alternate in dominance. Traditional reconstructions begin with roots completely unified in the Sphere under Love, trace Strife’s increasing separation through a cosmos like ours, reach complete segregation, and then follow Love’s return through a second cosmogony. Other readings question whether both transitions generate full worlds and living beings, how long complete endpoints last, and where particular zoogonical episodes belong. The Strasbourg papyrus strengthened evidence for cycling while also disrupting confident assignments of verses to a single direction of change.',
    'A responsible diagram would therefore display alternatives, not one certified wheel. The Sphere is divine, homogeneous, and motionless in some evidence, but it is not simply Parmenidean being, a monotheistic creator, or a final ethical heaven. Strife’s complete separation and Love’s complete unity are limiting states; the differentiated cosmos depends on their contest. Whether the present world forms under increasing Strife, increasing Love, or includes processes from both depends on reconstruction. What is secure is continual exchange among eternal roots under waxing and waning powers. Exact phase counts, duplicated creatures, and the placement of the daimonic exile remain interpretive questions.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['parmenides'], ['On Nature', 'Strasbourg papyrus']),
  section('biology', 'Bodies, organs, and experiments in natural explanation', [
    'Empedocles extends mixture theory into cosmogony and biology. Surviving material describes plants and animals, the emergence of separate limbs or strange combinations, the survival or persistence of viable wholes, sexual differentiation, embryology, breathing, and the structure of organs. Later summaries can make this look like one chronological evolutionary narrative, but fragment order and cosmic phase are disputed. The famous independent limbs are not straightforward ancestors of Darwinian natural selection. They show an attempt to explain organized bodies through combinations and failures within a cosmic process, without requiring that every form be designed whole from the start.',
    'Several reports also associate Empedocles with striking physical demonstrations or models: the clepsydra illustrates air and respiration, while accounts of pores and effluences explain bodily exchange. Such examples connect ordinary techniques with invisible processes, yet Aristotle or later commentators often supply the framing. Empedocles’s medicine cannot be reconstructed as a complete clinical school, nor should legendary cures certify scientific success. His lasting contribution is methodological breadth. The same roots and proportions are asked to explain tissues, organs, sensation, sex, and cosmic bodies, testing whether a small ontology can account for the diversity of life without new substances appearing from nothing.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-science', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['aristotle'], ['On Nature']),
  section('perception', 'Pores, effluences, likeness, and the limits of cognition', [
    'Perception occurs through physical interaction between perceiver and perceived. Objects give off effluences that fit pores in sense organs, and like elements in us recognize or respond to like elements outside. Empedocles offers particular accounts of vision, hearing, smell, and thought, while later witnesses organize them through their own psychological categories. The “like by like” formula is consequently an orientation, not a complete theory for every sense. Pore fit explains why not every effluence affects every organ; elemental kinship explains how contact becomes recognition. Both mechanisms remain corporeal and tied to the composition of an organism.',
    'Blood around the heart is prominent in cognition because its roots are especially balanced, but this need not identify thought with a modern organ or reduce every mental state to one recipe. Individuals understand according to their changing composition, and the poem urges disciplined attention beyond narrow mortal powers. That creates an epistemic tension: if cognition depends on material mixture, how can a poet claim unusually comprehensive insight? Invocation of the Muse, instruction, repeated observation, and purification offer partial answers. Empedocles neither rejects the senses nor treats them as transparent. Knowledge is embodied, proportion-dependent, and educable, while the surviving fragments do not yield a finished division between perception and reason.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['anaxagoras', 'aristotle'], ['On Nature']),
  section('daimones-ethics', 'Exile, transmigration, sacrifice, and living kinship', [
    'Religious fragments describe a daimōn exiled from blessed company after bloodshed or perjury and wandering through different mortal forms. Empedocles’s poetic speaker includes himself within a long sequence of lives as male and female, plant, bird, fish, and other creatures. These passages support transmigration and an expanded kinship among living beings, but their relation to physical roots is disputed. A daimōn may be a long-lived compound, a divine identity expressed through successive bodies, or something the extant physics cannot fully explain. The text should be allowed to expose that pressure rather than solved with an invented soul-substance.',
    'Condemnations of animal killing, meat consumption, and blood sacrifice follow from pollution and kinship: the victim may be a relative in another form, and violence perpetuates exile. Exact dietary rules and ritual practices remain fragmentary, and modern labels such as vegan, environmentalist, or pacifist carry institutions and motives not securely his. The ethical force is nonetheless substantial. Cosmic composition does not make organisms morally interchangeable; it makes apparent boundaries unstable and killing perilous. Love is thus not merely a cosmic mixer, yet neither can every religious command be derived mechanically from its physical action. Ethical and cosmic vocabularies overlap without becoming one smooth doctrine.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['pythagoras'], ['Purifications']),
  section('unity-tensions', 'How far physics and purification belong together', [
    'A strict two-world picture once made Empedocles appear divided between rational natural science and irrational religion. That judgment imposes modern disciplinary values and ignores shared language of mixture, kinship, love, strife, mortality, and divine aspiration. The Strasbourg evidence makes complete separation harder to defend, and one-poem interpretations show how daimonic history may unfold within cosmic cycles. Yet thematic connection does not prove one original roll, one addressee, one chronological order, or one mechanism explaining everything. Ancient title testimony and differences of voice still give two-poem approaches evidence to address.',
    'The strongest reading strategy is architectural humility. Place physical and religious fragments in conversation, test proposed links, and record where they strain. The roots explain bodily composition; they do not obviously explain the persistence of a responsible daimōn. Love joins unlike materials; ethical love condemns violence and seeks restored community, but cosmic total unity can eliminate organisms. Strife causes separation; moral wrongdoing also produces exile, yet the causal link is obscure. Empedocles may intentionally connect these domains through poetry rather than a modern deduction. Coherence can be thematic and practical without becoming a seamless system whose missing steps an editor supplies.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['On Nature', 'Purifications', 'Strasbourg papyrus']),
  section('reception-reading', 'Aristotle, later elemental theory, and a careful reading path', [
    'Aristotle made Empedocles central to later histories of four elements and causes, while criticizing the roots, the operation of Love and Strife, perception by likeness, and allegedly inconsistent explanations. His evidence is indispensable but translates poetic roots and powers into Aristotelian categories. Later medicine, Epicurean and Stoic debate, Plutarch, Neoplatonists, and Christian writers preserved lines for new purposes. The four-element scheme subsequently became more regular and durable than Empedocles’s own root theology. Reception therefore both saved and remodeled him, just as the Etna story converted a contested philosophical persona into a memorable death legend.',
    'Begin with a modern edition that prints transmitter, textual status, and concordances. Read B6, B8–B9, B17, B26, B35, B62, B84, B100, B107–B109, and the major purification fragments alongside Strasbourg ensembles, checking another numbering system before taking sequence for granted. Then compare the SEP overview, Inwood’s one-project reconstruction, and Wright’s more traditional organization. Read Aristotle last as witness and critic. Keep four columns—direct verse, papyrus sequence, testimony, reconstruction—and ask where roots, forces, cycles, bodies, cognition, and exile genuinely connect. The resulting Empedocles is more difficult than a four-element slogan and more coherent than a split personality.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'metaphysics', 'ethics'], ['aristotle', 'gorgias'], ['On Nature', 'Purifications', 'Strasbourg papyrus']),
];

