const anaxagorasSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'Mixture ordered by mind, known through mediated remains', [
    'Anaxagoras of Clazomenae describes a cosmos that begins not from nothing but from an undifferentiated mixture in which all ingredients are present. Nous—mind or intellect—initiates rotation, knows the mixture, and has power over the separating process. Perceptible objects emerge as ingredients become differently concentrated and predominant, although complete separation is never achieved among ordinary stuffs. This theory answers the problem of generation without reducing nature to four roots or indivisible atoms. It also creates its own puzzles: what exactly are the ingredients, how literally is everything in everything, and how extensively does Nous direct events after motion begins?',
    'The answers must respect unequal evidence. A small set of prose fragments survives chiefly because Simplicius quoted Anaxagoras roughly a millennium later. Plato and Aristotle preserve early and philosophically rich reactions; Theophrastean and doxographical traditions report perception and astronomy; biographers narrate an Athenian career, prosecution, and exile. These are not one continuous text. “Seeds,” “stuffs,” “opposites,” and “homoeomeries” belong to different words or interpretive layers. Anaxagoras is best approached as a subtle pluralist whose own formulations can be separated from the later causal histories that made him a failed teleologist or a heroic martyr for science.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['parmenides', 'plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Phaedo']),
  section('transmission', 'One lost book, fragments, and testimony', [
    'Ancient sources associate Anaxagoras with one prose book, and Plato’s Apology imagines it cheaply available in Socrates’ Athens. Only portions survive. Simplicius explicitly quotes from the work while commenting on Aristotle’s Physics, preserving the principal fragments on the original mixture, division without a smallest, dominance, and Nous. A fragment’s wording is stronger evidence than Simplicius’s explanation around it, though text, punctuation, and extraction still depend on editors. Traditional DK 59 B numbers remain common; newer collections reorder the dossier, so citations should name the edition or provide concordance rather than treating “fragment 4” as universally self-identifying.',
    'Plato and Aristotle stand closer in time but write to advance their own arguments. The Phaedo’s Socrates expects Nous to explain what is best, then criticizes Anaxagoras for relying on material conditions. Aristotle treats him within a history of causes, sometimes praising the introduction of intellect and sometimes charging inconsistency. Their evidence can reveal doctrines and early reception, but their disappointment cannot replace the fragments. Later reports about astronomy, biology, and perception are more mediated still. A formal review therefore cites exact Platonic and Bekker divisions, labels Simplicius as transmitter, and avoids turning a doxographical technical term into Anaxagoras’s own vocabulary.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Phaedo', 'Metaphysics']),
  section('life-athens', 'Clazomenae, Athens, Lampsacus, and uncertain chronology', [
    'Anaxagoras came from Clazomenae in Ionian Asia Minor and was remembered as an important natural philosopher active at Athens before the mature careers of Socrates and Plato. His conventional dates, about 500 to 428 BCE, and a thirty-year Athenian residence derive from attempts to coordinate conflicting testimony. Proposed arrival dates range widely, and no report securely documents a meeting with Socrates. Association with Pericles is plausible and important to reception, but descriptions such as teacher, friend, adviser, or dependent carry different evidential burdens. His thought’s presence at Athens is firmer than a precise institutional relationship.',
    'Ancient sources also disagree about prosecution, accusers, charges, sentence, and timing. Impiety connected with natural accounts of the sun and moon, political attack through Pericles, Persian sympathies, and other motives appear in different combinations. Departure to Lampsacus and later honor there have a credible traditional outline, but “trial and exile” should remain shorthand for uncertain stories rather than a reconstructed court record. The modern image of the first scientist persecuted by religion oversimplifies Athenian law, politics, and ancient natural theology. Biography can safely say that later memory made his cosmology politically and religiously consequential; it cannot supply a single certain narrative.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'political-philosophy'], ['socrates'], ['Fragments']),
  section('no-generation', 'No absolute birth or death: mixture and separation', [
    'Anaxagoras rejects the ordinary language of coming-to-be and perishing as literally misleading. Existing things combine and separate; nothing simply originates from nonbeing or vanishes into it. This position belongs among fifth-century pluralist responses to Eleatic pressure, although direct dependence on a particular Parmenidean passage is reconstructed rather than documented. Observable birth and death remain real processes at the level of composites. Their explanation changes: a new body is a new concentration and organization of ingredients that were already present, while decomposition redistributes them without annihilation.',
    'Empedocles achieves a related result with four roots, and atomists with indivisible bodies and void. Anaxagoras instead preserves an indefinitely divisible mixture containing every relevant kind. That makes qualitative transformation possible without deriving flesh from ingredients wholly lacking flesh or the hot from a region wholly lacking heat. Yet it also threatens explanatory excess: if everything is present everywhere, what makes one thing genuinely different from another? The answer involves proportions, predominance, and the range of separation. These concepts must carry the work that exclusive material composition performs in other pluralisms, and interpreters dispute how precisely they do so.',
  ], ['metaphysics'], ['parmenides', 'empedocles', 'leucippus', 'democritus'], ['Fragments']),
  section('ingredients', 'Everything in everything: stuffs, qualities, and seeds', [
    'The phrase “everything in everything” summarizes several fragments but does not define the inventory. Anaxagoras names opposites such as hot and cold, bright and dark, wet and dry, and mentions earth and unlimited seeds unlike one another. Later reports emphasize flesh, bone, hair, and other so-called homoeomerous stuffs. Scholars consequently debate whether fundamentals are qualities, stuffs, infinitely many substance-kinds, or a structured combination of these. “Seed” occurs in a fragment, but calling all ingredients tiny seeds can falsely atomize a theory that denies smallest parts. Aristotle’s term homoeomeries is particularly dangerous when presented as Anaxagoras’s own settled category.',
    'A food example preserved in later discussion captures the motivation: if nourishment becomes hair, flesh, and bone, relevant ingredients must already be available rather than generated from what is not. This need not mean miniature hairs hidden in bread. A stuff interpretation treats portions as indefinitely divisible quantities of kinds, while quality interpretations emphasize powers or opposites. Curd argues for ingredients as real stuffs characterized by their natures, but alternatives remain live. The secure core is abundance and non-exclusivity: the manifest thing does not contain only what it looks like, and transformation changes concentration and organization rather than introducing an entirely absent reality.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['aristotle'], ['Fragments']),
  section('division-dominance', 'No smallest, no purity, and the rule of predominance', [
    'Anaxagoras says there is no smallest among the small, because there is always a smaller, and no greatest without a greater. Division does not arrive at indivisible particles. Every portion of mixed matter, however small, continues to contain ingredients, so ordinary stuffs are never completely isolated “with an axe.” This is not modern mathematical proof of infinite divisibility, and translating portions as particles can suggest discrete units the theory denies. It is a metaphysical constraint on separation: processes redistribute and concentrate, but they do not purify a region into one ordinary ingredient alone.',
    'Predominance explains naming and appearance. A thing is called gold, flesh, or earth according to what is most evident or concentrated there, not because every other ingredient is absent. The claim need not mean that one ingredient is an absolute majority; dominance may be relative to perceptual and causal context. This allows stable differences within universal mixture, yet the fragments leave hard questions about thresholds, local proportions, and how one kind becomes manifest. The theory teaches an important epistemic caution: visible character is selective evidence about an internally richer composition, while rational explanation should not erase the genuine patterns that perception detects.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['democritus'], ['Fragments']),
  section('nous', 'Nous as unmixed knower and initiator of rotation', [
    'Nous differs sharply from the mixture. Anaxagoras describes it as unlimited, self-ruling, unmixed, finest and purest, possessing complete knowledge and greatest power. It knows what was mixed, what is separating, and what will be, and it initiates the cosmic rotation. Those claims make Nous causally and cognitively exceptional, but not straightforwardly a later immaterial creator God. “Finest” and “purest” can sound material, while its unmixed autonomy distinguishes it from ordinary stuffs. Whether Anaxagoras has a mind–matter dualism depends on which criteria count as matter and should not be announced without qualification.',
    'The fragments also say that Nous is present in living things, apparently in greater and lesser cases while remaining of one kind. Interpreters debate whether cosmic and individual mind are portions, manifestations, or activities of the same reality, and how an unmixed Nous can interact with mixture without becoming mixed. It need not create the ingredients or choose the best possible cosmos; its securely attested action begins and governs rotation and discrimination. Calling it “ordering” is justified, but “purposive designer” adds a stronger teleology. Anaxagoras’s originality lies in giving knowing, independent agency a cosmic causal role while leaving its exact ontology difficult.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Phaedo']),
  section('cosmogony', 'Rotation, separation, and a still-expanding cosmos', [
    'Before rotation, all ingredients were together and no familiar quality or object was manifest. Nous begins motion in a small region; the whirl extends and separates dense from rare, cold from hot, dark from bright, moist from dry. Air and aither move outward, while denser material contributes to earth, water, clouds, and stones. Separation is comparative rather than absolute because ingredients remain in one another. The fragments present a process continuing beyond its initial stage, and some interpretations allow an expanding ordered region within a still-unseparated mixture. Exact geometry and the relation of multiple vortices or worlds remain disputed.',
    'Anaxagoras’s astronomy became famous: later sources report an earthy moon, a fiery or red-hot stony sun and stars, reflected lunar light, eclipses, meteors, and celestial bodies carried by rotation. Some explanations were impressive and some incorrect by later standards; not all are preserved in his own words. Naturalizing the heavens did not necessarily deny divinity in a modern secular sense. It replaced or supplemented anthropomorphic stories with physical composition and motion. The philosophical importance lies in explanatory reach, while the details must retain testimony labels and must not be turned into a triumphalist list of scientific “firsts” whose priority and accuracy criteria go unnamed.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-science', 'metaphysics'], ['anaximander', 'anaximenes'], ['Fragments']),
  section('life-perception', 'Living beings, perception by contrast, and cognitive limits', [
    'Later testimony attributes to Anaxagoras accounts of plants as living, the origin and development of animals, respiration, sensation, and intelligence. Much of this material reaches us through Theophrastean or Aristotelian traditions rather than verbatim fragments. Perception is often summarized as occurring by opposites: what is unlike an organ produces a noticeable alteration, with excessively intense contacts causing pain. This contrasts with Empedoclean likeness, but the opposition can be too neat because reporters organize early views into competing families. Individual sensory mechanisms should therefore be cited as testimony rather than a recovered chapter of the book.',
    'Human perception cannot disclose every ingredient because mixture is indefinitely complex and small concentrations escape discrimination. A reported maxim that appearances provide a glimpse of what is obscure fits this evidential relation, though its exact status and translation require care. Reasoning extends beyond the dominant qualities that senses register, yet Nous’s complete knowledge is not automatically possessed by human thinkers. Hands, experience, memory, skill, and intelligence receive roles in later reports about human superiority. The profile can identify an epistemology of limited but informative appearance without claiming a complete Anaxagorean theory of inference or assigning every Theophrastean detail equal textual authority.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'philosophy-of-mind', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['empedocles', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments']),
  section('plato-aristotle', 'The teleological disappointment and its limits', [
    'In Plato’s Phaedo, Socrates recalls delight on hearing that Nous orders and causes all things. He expected the book to explain why each arrangement is best, but found air, aither, water, and other material factors doing most explanatory work. Plato uses this disappointment to distinguish a cause or reason from conditions without which an action could not occur. The passage is decisive for the history of teleology, but it reports Socrates’s expectation and Platonic argument. It does not quote Anaxagoras promising that every detail is selected for the good, nor prove that his actual explanatory aims were inconsistent.',
    'Aristotle develops the criticism by comparing Anaxagoras to a sober voice who then uses Nous as a device only when other explanations fail. Yet Aristotle also preserves indispensable material and recognizes a causal innovation. Modern readers should ask whether Nous’s intelligence entails deliberative choice, whether initiating a differentiating motion already counts as order, and whether local material explanations compete with or implement minded causation. The fragments secure knowledge and power for Nous more clearly than goodness. Calling Anaxagoras a failed teleologist accepts Plato’s target too quickly; calling him a complete mechanist ignores the exceptional cognitive cause he deliberately introduced.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['socrates', 'plato', 'aristotle'], ['Phaedo', 'Metaphysics']),
  section('rivals-reading', 'Pluralist alternatives and a source-layered reading path', [
    'Anaxagoras, Empedocles, and atomists preserve enduring realities beneath observable generation, but their inventories and mechanisms differ. Empedocles limits roots to four and adds Love and Strife; Anaxagoras permits indefinitely many ingredients in every mixture and grants Nous an initiating role; atomists use indivisibles and void. Aristotle’s comparisons make the family resemblance vivid while sometimes translating each into his own matter-and-cause scheme. These were not merely imperfect stages approaching Aristotle or modern science. They are rival answers to how qualitative difference, motion, knowledge, and stable explanation remain possible after challenges to coming-to-be.',
    'Begin with fragments B1–B6 and B8–B14 in Curd’s edition, noting when Simplicius supplies context. Diagram original mixture, rotation, relative separation, predominance, and Nous without drawing atoms. Then read SEP’s competing ingredient models and Curd’s essays. Add Phaedo 96a–99d and Aristotle Metaphysics 1.3–4 only as marked reception. Finish with Diogenes Book 2 while listing contradictions in arrival, prosecution, and exile stories. This layered route supports a precise summary: Anaxagoras posits universal, indefinitely divisible mixture and an exceptional knowing mover, while the inventory of stuffs, extent of teleology, cosmic chronology, and biography remain disputed.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'metaphysics'], ['empedocles', 'leucippus', 'democritus', 'plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments', 'Phaedo', 'Metaphysics']),
];

